(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-soap-case-presentations-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"SOAP Note and Case Presentations — Physical Diagnosis 1 Exam 3","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the sample note say the chief complaint should be recorded?","choices":["Translated into the correct diagnostic terminology","In the patient's own words","As the single system most likely to be involved","As the code that will be submitted for the visit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The sample note labels the chief complaint as being in the patient's own words. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the sample note, how does the patient describe the severity of her right cheek pain?","choices":["It began at 5 out of 10 and has fallen to 3 out of 10","It has remained at 7 out of 10 since it began","It began at 3 out of 10 and is currently 5 out of 10","It has never exceeded 2 out of 10 at any point"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The note records painful pressure starting at 3 out of 10 and currently 5 out of 10. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which manoeuvres does the sample note say increase the patient's pain to 7 out of 10?","choices":["Lying flat, coughing, and holding her breath","Standing up quickly, walking, and climbing stairs","Turning her head, swallowing, and speaking","Leaning her head down, smiling, and chewing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note names these three as raising the pain to 7 out of 10. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the sample note anchor the top of the patient's pain scale?","choices":["A 10 for her is childbirth","A 10 for her is a previous migraine","A 10 for her is a fractured limb","The note leaves the top of the scale undefined"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The note records that 10 out of 10 for this patient is childbirth, which is exactly the anchoring the pain mnemonics call for. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the sample note record about the patient's use of an over-the-counter medication?","choices":["She stopped it after a single dose because of an adverse effect","She has taken it for four days but cannot provide dosage information","She has never taken any medication for these symptoms","She was prescribed it by another clinician last week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The note records two tablets by mouth once daily for four days, that she cannot give the dosage, and that she reports following the package instructions. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why does the patient in the sample note believe her recent symptoms differ from her usual seasonal allergies?","choices":["Because the symptoms began at an unusual time of the year for her","Because her family members developed the same symptoms at the same time","Because she has developed both a cough and shortness of breath","Because the facial pain and thicker mucus differ from her usual symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note contrasts her initial itchy and watery eyes and clear mucus with the new facial pain and thick yellow-green mucus. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which symptoms does the sample note explicitly record the patient as denying?","choices":["Nasal congestion, facial pressure, measured fever","Sore throat, headache, ear pain, breathlessness, cough","Itchy eyes, watery eyes, repeated sneezing","Facial pain, thick mucus, feeling feverish"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These pertinent negatives are recorded alongside her denial of sick contacts at home or work. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does the sample note record in the family history?","choices":["A parent with chronic sinus disease requiring surgery","No family history of any relevance to the presentation","Similar seasonal allergies in siblings","A sibling treated recently for pneumonia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The note records the family history as significant for similar seasonal allergies in her siblings. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What temperature is recorded in the sample note's objective section?","choices":["36.5 degrees Celsius orally","37 degrees Celsius tympanically","39.4 degrees Celsius rectally","38 degrees Celsius orally"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note records 38 degrees Celsius orally, alongside blood pressure 120/68, heart rate 76, respiratory rate 16, and oxygen saturation 99 percent on room air. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the sample note record on palpation of the sinuses?","choices":["Both the maxillary and the frontal sinuses are tender to palpation","Only the right maxillary sinus is tender, with the left unaffected","Maxillary sinuses tender bilaterally, frontal sinuses not tender","All of the sinuses are entirely nontender to palpation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The note separates the two sinus groups in exactly this way. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the sample note record about sinus transillumination?","choices":["Transillumination is present on both sides","Transillumination is absent on both sides","Transillumination was not attempted at this visit","Absent on the right, present on the left"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note pairs the absent right-sided transillumination with the right-sided maxillary findings. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does the sample note describe the tympanic membranes?","choices":["Erythematous and bulging bilaterally with loss of the light reflex","Pearly grey with a sharp light reflex, no visible middle ear fluid","Retracted bilaterally, with visible fluid behind both membranes","Not visualised because of obstructing cerumen in both canals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The note also records that the ear canals are clear bilaterally. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the sample note describe within the nasal passages?","choices":["Clear thin mucus on both sides with entirely normal turbinates","Thick green mucus on the left with a visibly deviated septum","Thick green mucus on the right with erythematous, edematous turbinates","Bleeding from both nasal passages with visible crusting throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The note adds no drainage on the left, with mild erythema and edema of the left turbinates. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the sample note record on examination of the neck?","choices":["Tender anterior cervical lymph nodes with the trachea still midline","A deviated trachea with visible masses in the anterior neck","An enlarged thyroid gland without any associated lymphadenopathy","No masses, trachea midline, no cervical lymphadenopathy or thyromegaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the neck examination as the sample note documents it. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does the sample note describe the thorax and lungs?","choices":["Symmetrical expansion, resonant to percussion, clear to auscultation","Dull to percussion at both bases with coarse crackles on inspiration","Asymmetrical chest expansion with visible accessory muscle use","Wheezing throughout all of the lung fields on expiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The note also records no visible masses or deformities, no accessory muscle use, and no tenderness to palpation. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the sample note record on cardiac examination?","choices":["An irregular rhythm with an audible third heart sound at the apex","A systolic murmur best heard at the apex on expiration","Regular rate and rhythm, crisp first and second sounds, nothing added","A pericardial rub with marked tenderness over the precordium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The note also records no lifts or heaves and no tenderness or thrills over the precordium. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which condition appears in the sample note's differential diagnoses?","choices":["Bacterial meningitis","Retained nasal foreign body","Dental abscess","Temporomandibular joint dysfunction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The differential lists acute sinusitis, allergic rhinitis, viral upper respiratory infection, and retained nasal foreign body. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the sample note require alongside each differential diagnosis listed?","choices":["The estimated probability of it, expressed as a percentage","The name of the single test that would confirm it definitively","A brief statement correlating her findings with that potential diagnosis","A citation to the relevant published clinical practice guideline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sample note marks each of the four entries with exactly this requirement. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are the two numbered diagnoses in the sample note's assessment?","choices":["Viral upper respiratory infection and retained nasal foreign body","Acute sinusitis and viral upper respiratory infection","Allergic rhinitis alone, listed as a single problem","Acute sinusitis first, then allergic rhinitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The assessment lists acute sinusitis as number one and allergic rhinitis as number two. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is the fluticasone regimen written in the sample note's plan?","choices":["One spray each nostril twice daily for seven days, then once daily","Two sprays in each nostril once daily for a total of fourteen days","One spray in the affected nostril only, three times every day","One spray in each nostril once daily, continued indefinitely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The note gives fluticasone 50 micrograms nasal spray on exactly this schedule for nasal allergy symptoms. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What patient education accompanies the nasal spray in the sample plan?","choices":["To refrigerate the spray in between each of the doses","To stop the spray immediately if any nasal dryness develops","To rinse the mouth out thoroughly after each dose","Proper spray technique, with a caution against spraying the septum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The note documents both the technique education and the specific septal caution. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What follow-up instruction does the sample plan give?","choices":["Return in six months for a routine scheduled review","Return only if a fever above 39 degrees Celsius develops at home","Follow up if symptoms have not improved in five days, sooner if worse","No follow-up is required once the prescription has been collected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The plan gives both the interval and the instruction to return sooner for anything new, worsening, or otherwise concerning. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does the sample plan close the encounter?","choices":["By recording that the assessment and plan were discussed with the patient, who understood and agreed","By recording that a printed copy of the instructions was mailed to the patient","By recording that the patient declined the treatment that was proposed","By recording that a family member was asked to supervise the treatment course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The note documents both the discussion and the patient's understanding and agreement. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does the lecture define an oral case presentation?","choices":["A verbatim reading of the written note to the supervising clinician on duty","A well organised vignette describing the patient and the problem","A summary limited to the final diagnosis and the medication prescribed","A discussion of the relevant published literature supporting the diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that it helps listeners visualise the patient and understand the problem. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which situation does the lecture list as an occasion for making a presentation?","choices":["Documenting the encounter in the electronic record","Explaining the diagnosis to the patient's family at the bedside","Presenting to consulting practitioners","Teaching a formal seminar to a group of students"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists preceptors and supervising physicians, consulting practitioners, rounds, and phone calls. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which four things does the lecture say an oral presentation conveys?","choices":["The patient's demographics, insurance, referral source, and appointment record","The diagnosis, the prognosis, the cost, and the expected total length of stay","The history, the examination, the literature, and the published guidelines","What the patient told you, what you found, what you concluded, what you plan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives these four alongside the rule that only information relevant to the patient's condition is included. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"In what order does the lecture say an oral presentation should run?","choices":["The same order as written reports","Beginning with the assessment, so the listener knows the conclusion first","In the order the information was obtained during the encounter","Beginning with the physical examination findings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture then lists that order, from chief complaint and history of present illness through to assessment and plan. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How much does the lecture say to include for an established patient being seen in clinic?","choices":["The complete medical history, exactly as for a brand new patient","The chief complaint and any active problems","Only the medications and allergies","Only what has changed since the last visit, with no complaint restated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New patients get the complete medical history, while follow-up visits cover the status of active problems, any new concerns, and changes since the previous evaluation. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which pearl does the lecture offer for delivering a presentation?","choices":["Include every detail, since listeners cannot ask questions afterwards","Read directly from your notes so that nothing at all is omitted","Present the findings in whatever order they happen to come to mind","Tell a story, and try to make it interesting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pearls are to stay relaxed, keep everything in order, avoid reading your notes, tell an interesting story, and err on the side of saying less. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the lecture's closing summary say about the words normal and abnormal?","choices":["They are acceptable provided they are used consistently through the note","They should be reserved for use in the assessment section","They do not give enough detail; say what they mean for that patient","They are preferred because they help keep the note concise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The summary asks you to be specific, since normal or abnormal alone does not convey the finding. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
